  "question": "Do direct visual images of the NLC-backed FID 412004000070 object expose a readable Tieqin Tongjian Lou or Huang Pilei / Baishong Yichan provenance seal sufficient to close identity with Zhang Lijuan's Tieqin-old-collection Yuan-impression copy?",
  "answer": "Not yet. Direct, hash-bound visual review of FID070 book 1 establishes that its front/title leaf (PDF page 2) visibly carries pale provenance-seal impressions beneath and around the dark title/volume inscription. The seal traces are real physical marks, but the impressions are faded and overprinted/obscured by the black title inscription, so neither 《百宋一廛》, 《鐵琴銅劍樓》 nor another Qu-family seal can be read or uniquely matched with sufficient confidence. Strategic front and terminal leaves from books 2, 16 and 32 were independently rendered and visually reviewed; no directly readable target provenance seal was found in those reviewed leaves. Authoritative comparator sources confirm what the expected Huang and Tieqin seals look like and how they are cataloged, but shape-level similarity is not promoted into a target-object identification. Therefore the project advances from 'digital object not visually inspected for provenance' to 'provenance marks visually present but inscription unresolved'. FID070 == Zhang Lijuan's exact Tieqin physical copy remains STRONGLY_COMPATIBLE_NOT_PROVED; no same-object edge is authorized.",
